The Cosmic Dawn
2:37
The tiny ripples we see in the cosmic microwave background—those faint temperature and density variations—are the fingerprints of increasing complexity. Within 100-300 million years of the Big Bang, stars began to light up unevenly across the early Universe. The Universe just got a big glow-up.
Threshold 2:
Stars Light Up
How stars are born.
Where Do Stars Come From?
Stars are born in nebulae, huge clouds of gas and dust that collect over millions of years, thanks to slight variations in gravity. These clouds can be hundreds of light-years across. Gas and dust clump together, drawing in more matter into denser clumps as gravity increases. As these compacted clumps of hydrogen and helium grow denser, they heat up to form a plasma, a sort of hot, swirling soup made up of free-floating atomic nuclei. At high enough temperatures, free-floating nuclei slam together with such intensity that they fuse into a new element. This process is called
nuclear fusion
.
