Supercluster
Until the 1980s, astronomers considered galaxy clusters to be the largest structures in the Universe. But larger structures, called superclusters, were soon revealed. These strands of tens of thousands of galaxies link the Universe together in a cosmic web. This image of Supercluster Abell 901/902 uses a special filter to identify where astronomers believe dark matter is located. Dark matter is an invisible type of matter that we don't really understand, but astronomers believe it accounts for 85% of all mass in the Universe. Dark matter's gravity is the scaffolding that holds the superstructures of the Universe together.
